WebKit: a scroll repaints only what fixed layers paint, and the page's own display rate is capped.

Two things made a scroll on a news page (Ars Technica) repaint the whole view on every step:

* scrollContentsFastPath repaints the rect of every fixed (viewport-constrained) layer. A page often has
  fixed layers that cover the whole view and paint nothing themselves (an overlay's container, a consent
  dialog's frame, an ad slot), so the "fast path" repainted everything. On LazyOS only the rects of the
  layers in such a layer's non-composited subtree that paint something are repainted (a superset of what
  is painted, never less).

* An animation or a ticker displays as often as the drawing area is acknowledged, which on one CPU took
  the time a scroll needed. A display that does not carry a scroll now waits until
  LAZYOS_WEBKIT_DISPLAY_INTERVAL_MS (33 ms, 0 to 1000) after the last one; a display that carries a scroll
  is never held back (the offset is set before scheduleDisplay, and a held-back display is brought
  forward by one), and the overscan bands of patch 0030 may be painted while a display is held back.

--- a/Source/WebCore/page/LocalFrameView.cpp
